title The falsifier needs a wide grid, not a big one -- the cache boundary is a row length, and the toolchain is already in the tree
prompt Diffuser lap under the baton: read the inner prompt and the card, find the next self-generated fruit or say the lane is fallow.
think The last lap left one open door: a compiled sweep kernel, which it read as blocked on a compiler. No cc, gcc, or clang is on the path.
think The tree vendors vendor/zig-toolchain/zig, which reports 0.16.0, so the compiler block does not hold. The new instrument's real blocker is the claim and the ruling.
think The falsifier names a cache in lines, and the machine has a cache in bytes. Five row bands cross the 16 MiB L3 at 838,860 cells per row.
think A square grid that wide is about 2.8 TB, far past the 15.9 GB here. A wide grid of eight or sixteen rows fits, at about 25 to 51 MiB.
think So the translation the paper called impossible is a change of grid shape. The L2 arm, at 26,214 cells per row, needs only tens of megabytes.
think Which arm is testable depends on which cache level the cache-misses event counts. That is unknown here and goes first in the calibration.
obs The computed grid sizes are arithmetic from the guest's cache report, not a measurement. The guest's shared L3 may not be the capacity this VM can use.
obs No instrument was built and no claim was opened. The paper names the claim as the next door, for Keaton's or Claude's ruling.
rota lap 8701, row 1 -- air feels: the reading surfaced a wrong premise, that the toolchain was missing, and the wide grid as the way across the boundary
orbit round 1 Aries -- fire talent: commence and finish one to-do; the to-do was this check-in
